swedish home style cooking with a  sophisticated twist that's frederick  anders passion the chef uses all kinds  of fresh herbs to put a special spin on  traditional ingredients  like veal  and asparagus  and to garnish desserts like rhubarb  sorbet  around 20 different herbs are used in  his kitchen including verbena cress and  thai lemon basil  the restaurant provent swears by fresh  ingredients and most products are  delivered every morning  it's good it tastes good  number f  there's nothing like a fresh herb or a  really fresh salad because you can feel  i mean if it's  newly picked or really fresh then you  can feel the  would sound cliche to say that you can  feel the sunlight but then you can feel  the green in it always that's where you  can really feel it when it's still taste  chlorophyll  then it's fresh  frederick sources many of his  ingredients from the company orton novo  a 45-minute drive from stockholm they  grow herbs and salads in huge  greenhouses all year round here alone  there are seven different types of basil  but also red  and yellow charred  two hundred thousand plants leave the  facility every week  and they're sent to restaurants across  the whole of sweden  when i began this in 88  we  in sweden more or less there were only  three  traditional fresh herbs and that was  the chives parsley and the deal  and  what they have been interested in is is  more more you know what is a little bit  different what is it what is special and  what can be um  you know what can add something to the  kitchen i think  so we have about 50 different varieties  and  the more the special more they are  interesting to the chefs  today frederick is making the most of  this wide variety to update a  traditional swedish dish fresh pike  dumplings with beetroot for the  dumplings we're using the ground the  ground pike  we're using salt  using a little bit of the whipped cream  and also  eggs  and with the dumplings we're going to  serve them with our bread beets fresh  red beets which we cook  with  a little bit of cumin water sugar and  salt  he mixes 600 grams of fresh pike  together with a pinch of salt and 500  grams of cream in a food processor until  the mixture is stiff  you have to be careful  not to not to mix it too long or not too  slow because it's it has to stick  together and if you mix it too long or  if you are  if you have it's too warm or something  then it can split then the ground and  the cream doesn't go together  form the mixture into dumplings with a  spoon and place them into water just  below the boil  so then we cook them three minutes turn  them over so you get them on the other  side as well and another three minute  three minutes on the other side  frederick mixes the beetroot together  with cumin seeds salt and a splash of  vinegar  he tests with a sharp prong to see  whether they're ready  and they are  we eat a lot of beets and root  vegetables so it's very that's very  traditional because that's what you can  grow  not all year round but you can grow it  and you can keep it in storage for the  winter because we have such  cold climates  the pike dumplings with beetroot are  served with a hollandaise sauce and  fresh herbs  red chard dill and watercress not only  enhance the flavor but they make the  dish more appealing to the eye  you
 

